<h1 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Overview">Overview</h1>
<p></p>
<p>NVIDIA's Triton Inference Server is a specialized backend for serving AI=
 models in production. It provides a standard gRPC/HTTP API that lets us av=
oid building custom deployment applications for each model. The server is a=
 comprehensive platform with built-in features like dynamic batching, concu=
rrent execution, and a flexible backend system that supports everything fro=
m Python to highly optimized TensorRT engines. Adopting Triton standardizes=
 our MLOps pipeline, replacing ad-hoc solutions with a scalable and optimiz=
ed system.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-SimplifiedIntegration"><str=
ong>Simplified Integration</strong></h3>
<p>Triton abstracts model complexity from the application layer. Our servic=
es don't need to know how a model is implemented; they just make a standard=
 gRPC call to a named endpoint. This decoupling is powerful. For example, i=
t made A/B testing FasterWhisper against Parakeet trivial. Since they share=
d an identical input/output signature within Triton, the business logic cou=
ld switch between them via a simple configuration change, requiring no code=
 redeployment.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-PerformanceandResourceOptim=
ization"><strong>Performance and Resource Optimization</strong></h3>
<p>The primary performance justification comes from the Gemma-3n implementa=
tion, which saw a <strong>3x speed increase</strong> and a <strong>2.5-3x r=
eduction in VRAM usage</strong> after moving to Triton. These gains stem fr=
om features that are complex to build and maintain in a custom service:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Dynamic Batching</strong>: Triton automatically groups incoming =
requests from different users into a single batch on the fly, which is one =
of the most effective ways to maximize GPU throughput.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Concurrent Model Execution</strong>: The server can intelligentl=
y schedule and run multiple models=E2=80=94or multiple instances of the sam=
e model=E2=80=94on a single GPU, overlapping operations to keep the hardwar=
e saturated.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Efficient Memory Management</strong>: Triton can load a single i=
nstance of model weights and allow many concurrent requests to share it. Th=
is drastically reduces the VRAM footprint compared to running multiple redu=
ndant Python processes in separate containers.</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-FlexibilityandMaintainabili=
ty"><strong>Flexibility and Maintainability</strong></h3>
<p>Triton's architecture solves several long-term maintenance challenges. I=
ts use of isolated backends resolves the "dependency hell" problem; each mo=
del runs in its own environment, so Gemma-3n's PyTorch version will never c=
onflict with Parakeet's NeMo dependencies.</p>
<p>This structure also provides a clear path for iterative optimization. We=
 can deploy a model quickly using the flexible Python backend and, if it be=
comes a bottleneck, rewrite its core in a high-performance backend like Ten=
sorRT. Throughout this process, the client-facing API remains unchanged, co=
mpletely isolating the optimization work from the business logic.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Observability"><strong>Obse=
rvability</strong></h3>
<p>Building robust monitoring for a custom service is a significant task. T=
riton provides this out of the box, exposing a Prometheus metrics endpoint =
with detailed data on GPU utilization, memory, queue times, and inference l=
atency for every model.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Gemma-3n">Gemma-3n</h1>
<p>This implementation serves Google's Gemma-3n vision-language model throu=
gh a highly optimized Triton Python backend that bypasses standard HuggingF=
ace preprocessing to maintain GPU-resident tensors throughout the entire pi=
peline. The key innovation is avoiding CPU roundtrips for image data that o=
ften exists in CUDA shared memory, providing significant performance improv=
ements for video analysis workloads.</p>
<p>The implementation uses the HuggingFace processor exclusively for chat t=
emplating and tokenization while handling all image preprocessing directly =
on GPU using native PyTorch operations. This approach eliminates the overhe=
ad of PIL/NumPy conversions and CPU-GPU memory transfers that plague standa=
rd vision-language model deployments.</p>
<p>Standard HuggingFace vision model implementations force image data throu=
gh a CPU preprocessing pipeline that includes PIL Image objects, NumPy arra=
y conversions, and processor normalization steps. This design assumes image=
s arrive as files or CPU arrays, which is inefficient when video frames are=
 already GPU-resident or transmitted through CUDA shared memory.</p>
<p>The implementation circumvents this entire preprocessing chain by:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Using HuggingFace processor only for text templating and tokenization</p=
></li>
<li>
<p>Implementing custom GPU-native image preprocessing with PyTorch operatio=
ns</p></li>
<li>
<p>Maintaining tensor memory layout optimizations throughout the pipeline</=
p></li>
<li>
<p>Supporting direct DLPack zero-copy transfers from CUDA shared memory</p>=
</li>
</ul>
<p>Video processing applications typically produce frames in NHWC format (N=
umber of frames, Height, Width, Channels) while GPU operations prefer NCHW =
format (Number of frames, Channels, Height, Width). The implementation hand=
les both layouts automatically, performing on-GPU permutations when necessa=
ry to avoid CPU involvement.<br></p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Input/OutputTensors"><stron=
g>Input/Output Tensors</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Input: </strong><code>PROMPT</code></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Shape</strong>: <code>[1, 1]</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: The user-provided text prompt for the mode=
l. The implementation defensively handles shapes of both</p>
<p><code>[1]</code> and <code>[1, 1]</code>.</p>
<p></p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Input: </strong><code>FRAMES</code></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_FP16</code>, <code>TYPE_FP32</code>, o=
r <code>TYPE_UINT8</code></p></li>
<li>
<p></p>
<p><strong>Shape</strong>: <code>[1, num_frames, height, width, 3]</code> (=
NHWC) or <code>[1, num_frames, 3, height, width]</code> (NCHW)</p>
<p></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: A tensor containing one or more video fram=
es. The implementation dynamically handles various input dimensions (3D, 4D=
, or 5D) and normalizes them for processing. It also automatically permutes=
 NHWC layouts to the GPU-preferred NCHW format.</p>
<p></p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Output: </strong><code>SUMMARY</code></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Shape</strong>: <code>[1]</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: The generated text describing the input fr=
ames.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Batching">Batching</h3>
<p>Triton's batching system requires all input tensors to include a leading=
 batch dimension, even for single requests. This affects client implementat=
ions:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>PROMPT tensor shape: <code>[batch_size, 1]</code> instead of <code>[1]</=
code></p></li>
<li>
<p>FRAMES tensor shape: <code>[batch_size, num_frames, height, width, 3]</c=
ode> or <code>[batch_size, num_frames, 3, height, width]</code></p></li>
<li>
<p>Batch size is typically 1 for individual requests</p></li>
</ul>
<p>The implementation handles variable batch dimensions by detecting the te=
nsor shape and extracting the actual content appropriately. PROMPT tensors =
may arrive as <code>[1]</code> or <code>[1,1]</code> depending on client co=
nfiguration, requiring defensive dimension handling.</p>
<p>Frame tensors arrive with varying dimensions depending on client impleme=
ntation:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>5D tensors: <code>[batch, frames, height, width, channels]</code> from b=
atched clients</p></li>
<li>
<p>4D tensors: <code>[frames, height, width, channels]</code> from direct c=
lients</p></li>
<li>
<p>3D tensors: <code>[height, width, channels]</code> from single-image cli=
ents</p></li>
</ul>
<p>The implementation normalizes all inputs to 4D format before processing:=
</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">if fram=
es.dim() =3D=3D 5:
    frames =3D frames.squeeze(0)  # Remove batch dimension
elif frames.dim() =3D=3D 3:
    frames =3D frames.unsqueeze(0)  # Add frame dimension
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-CUDASharedMemoryIntegration=
">CUDA Shared Memory Integration</h3>
<p>The most significant performance optimization involves direct CUDA share=
d memory access through DLPack protocol:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">try:
    dl =3D frames_input.to_dlpack()   # Zero-copy if from CUDA SHM
    frames =3D torch.from_dlpack(dl)
    dlpack_used =3D True
except Exception:
    # Fallback: CPU numpy copy then GPU transfer
    np_frames =3D frames_input.as_numpy()
    frames =3D torch.from_numpy(np_frames).to(device, non_blocking=3DTrue)
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>When clients use CUDA shared memory for frame data, DLPack enables zero-=
copy tensor creation directly on GPU. This eliminates:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>CPU memory allocation for intermediate arrays</p></li>
<li>
<p>GPU-to-CPU memory transfers</p></li>
<li>
<p>CPU-to-GPU memory transfers</p></li>
<li>
<p>NumPy array creation and destruction overhead</p></li>
</ul>
<p>The fallback path handles clients that provide CPU arrays, copying data =
to GPU with non-blocking transfers for optimal pipeline utilization.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-MediaProcessingPipeline">Me=
dia Processing Pipeline</h2>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Transformers"><br>
Transformers</h3>
<p>The primary motivation for custom text processing is to prevent unnecess=
ary memory transfers when image data already exists on GPU. The standard Hu=
ggingFace vision-language processing pipeline expects PIL Images or NumPy a=
rrays as inputs, which forces the following inefficient memory flow:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Client Preparation</strong>: Video frames exist in CUDA shared m=
emory on GPU</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Standard Pipeline Requirement</strong>: HuggingFace processor ex=
pects CPU-based PIL/NumPy image data</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Forced Transfer</strong>: GPU tensors must be moved to CPU, conv=
erted to PIL/NumPy format</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Re-upload</strong>: Processor converts back to PyTorch tensors a=
nd moves to GPU for model inference</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Performance Loss</strong>: Dual memory transfers plus CPU conver=
sion overhead</p></li>
</ol>
<p>The implementation bypasses this inefficient pipeline by handling image =
data directly as GPU tensors:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Direct CUDA Access</strong>: Accept image tensors through DLPack=
 from CUDA shared memory</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>GPU-Native Processing</strong>: Perform all normalization, resiz=
ing, and format conversion on GPU</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Text-Only Tokenization</strong>: Use HuggingFace processor exclu=
sively for chat template and text tokenization</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Manual Token Assembly</strong>: Combine text tokens with image p=
laceholder tokens without forcing CPU conversion</p></li>
</ul>
<p>This approach ensures image data never leaves GPU memory, eliminating th=
e redundant GPU=E2=86=92CPU=E2=86=92GPU transfer cycle that would otherwise=
 dominate processing time for video analysis workloads.</p>
<p>The implementation leverages HuggingFace's chat template system to forma=
t multi-modal conversations properly:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">message=
s =3D [
    {"role": "system", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": self.system_mes=
sage}]},
    {"role": "user", "content": ([{"type": "image"}] * num_imgs) + [{"type"=
: "text", "text": prompt}]}
]</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This structure places image placeholders before text content, matching G=
emma-3n's expected input format for vision-language understanding.</p>
<p>The implementation bypasses HuggingFace's automatic image token expansio=
n to maintain control over the tokenization process:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Template Generation</strong>: Create chat template without token=
ization to inspect structure</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Manual Tokenization</strong>: Tokenize template text to access r=
aw token IDs</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Token ID Detection</strong>: Locate image placeholder tokens and=
 soft token IDs</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Custom Expansion</strong>: Replace single image tokens with sequ=
ences of soft tokens</p></li>
</ol>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Repla=
ce each &lt;image&gt; with image_seq_length copies of &lt;image_soft_token&=
gt;
for t, m in zip(ids, mask):
    if t =3D=3D img_id:
        new_ids.extend([soft_id] * image_seq_length)
        new_mask.extend([m] * image_seq_length)
    else:
        new_ids.append(t)
        new_mask.append(m)</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This approach ensures proper alignment between image features and token =
positions while maintaining compatibility with Gemma-3n's vision encoder ou=
tput dimensions.</p>
<p>Gemma-3n's vision encoder produces a fixed number of features per image,=
 typically 256 tokens. This value is extracted from the model configuration=
:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">image_s=
eq_length =3D getattr(getattr(self.model.config, "vision_config", {}), "ima=
ge_seq_length", 256)</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>The sequence length determines how many soft tokens replace each image p=
laceholder, ensuring the text sequence aligns with vision encoder outputs d=
uring cross-attention operations.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Attention">Attention</h3>
<p>The implementation enables all available SDPA backends for optimal atten=
tion computation:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">os.envi=
ron["PYTORCH_SDP_KERNEL"] =3D "flash,mem_efficient,math"
torch.backends.cuda.enable_flash_sdp(True)
torch.backends.cuda.enable_mem_efficient_sdp(True)
torch.backends.cuda.enable_math_sdp(True)</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>Note: While this configuration prioritizes FlashAttention for memory eff=
iciency, currently it is not supported. <br>
We explicitly use cuDNN (CUDA Deep Neural Network library) is NVIDIA's hard=
ware-accelerated library that provides optimized implementations of deep le=
arning primitives. For attention mechanisms, cuDNN offers specialized kerne=
ls that can dramatically outperform generic matrix operations by leveraging=
 GPU architecture features like Tensor Cores, optimized memory access patte=
rns, and fused operations.</p>
<p>SDPA (Scaled Dot-Product Attention) represents the core attention comput=
ation in Transformers: <code>Attention(Q,K,V) =3D softmax(QK^T/=E2=88=9Ad)V=
</code>. When PyTorch's SDPA implementation detects compatible hardware and=
 software configurations, it can dispatch attention computations to cuDNN's=
 optimized kernels instead of using standard PyTorch operations.</p>
<p>The integration works through PyTorch's backend selection system:</p>
<p>When cuDNN kernels aren't compatible, we fall back to memory-efficient a=
ttention for compatibility, and maintains math attention as a final fallbac=
k to prevent "no available kernel" errors.</p>
<p>For Ampere and Hopper GPU architectures, TF32 precision provides signifi=
cant performance improvements:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">torch.s=
et_float32_matmul_precision("high")
torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32 =3D True
torch.backends.cudnn.allow_tf32 =3D True</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>TF32 maintains float32 dynamic range while using reduced precision for m=
atrix multiplication operations, typically providing 1.5-2x speedup on mode=
rn hardware.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-OutputTokens">Output Tokens=
</h3>
<p><br>
Output length is controlled through the <code>max_new_tokens</code> paramet=
er, defaulting to 320 tokens but configurable through Triton model paramete=
rs. This setting has significant performance implications due to the auto-r=
egressive nature of language model generation.</p>
<p><strong>Auto-regressive Generation Process:</strong></p>
<p>Language models generate text one token at a time in sequence, where eac=
h new token depends on all previously generated tokens. This creates a fund=
amentally sequential process that cannot be parallelized across the token d=
imension:</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>First Token</strong>: Model processes the entire input context (=
image features + text prompt) to generate token 1</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Second Token</strong>: Model processes the input context PLUS to=
ken 1 to generate token 2</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Third Token</strong>: Model processes the input context PLUS tok=
ens 1-2 to generate token 3</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Continue</strong>: This pattern repeats until max_new_tokens is =
reached or an end token is generated</p></li>
</ol>
<p>The <code>max_new_tokens</code> parameter acts as a hard ceiling on gene=
ration time because:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Generation time scales roughly linearly with the number of tokens produc=
ed</p></li>
<li>
<p>Each new token requires attention computation over the growing sequence =
length</p></li>
<li>
<p>Longer sequences require more memory reads for key-value cache access</p=
></li>
<li>
<p>The model must evaluate the stopping condition after each token</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Setting <code>max_new_tokens</code> too high creates several problems:</=
p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Even if the model could describe a scene in 50 tokens, setting max_new_t=
okens=3D1000 means clients must wait for up to 1000 generation steps if the=
 model doesn't produce an end token</p></li>
<li>
<p>Higher token limits require larger key-value cache allocations, reducing=
 concurrent request capacity</p></li>
<li>
<p>Long generation sequences increase the probability of hitting client tim=
eout limits</p></li>
<li>
<p>The model may continue generating redundant or hallucinated content beyo=
nd the useful description length</p></li>
</ul>
<p>The default of 320 tokens (roughly ~1300 characters) represents a balanc=
e between descriptive capability and performance. Unlike the vision encoder=
 which processes all image patches in parallel, or the initial forward pass=
 which processes the entire input context simultaneously, token generation =
is inherently sequential. This means:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Vision Processing</strong>: All image features computed in singl=
e forward pass (parallel)</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Initial Text Processing</strong>: Entire prompt processed simult=
aneously (parallel)</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Token Generation</strong>: Each output token requires separate f=
orward pass (sequential)</p></li>
</ul>
<p>This architectural constraint makes token limit selection one of the mos=
t critical performance tuning parameters for auto-regressive vision-languag=
e models.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-OtherImplementationNotes">O=
ther Implementation Notes</h2>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-GreedyDecodingStrategy">Gre=
edy Decoding Strategy</h3>
<p>The implementation enforces deterministic output through greedy decoding=
 configuration:</p>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: bash; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence">gc.do_s=
ample =3D False      # Disable sampling randomness
gc.num_beams =3D 1         # Single beam for deterministic output
gc.top_p =3D 1.0           # Disable nucleus sampling
gc.temperature =3D 1.0     # Neutral temperature</pre>
</div>
</div>
<p>This configuration ensures reproducible results for identical inputs, wh=
ich is crucial for testing and production deployments where consistent beha=
vior is required.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-MemoryEfficiencyOptimizatio=
ns">Memory Efficiency Optimizations</h3>
<p>The implementation minimizes memory usage through several strategies:</p=
>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>bfloat16 Precision</strong>: Reduces activation memory by 50% co=
mpared to float32</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>In-place Operations</strong>: Uses division operations that modi=
fy tensors in-place where possible</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Gradient Disabled</strong>: <code>@torch.inference_mode()</code>=
 eliminates gradient computation overhead</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Efficient Layouts</strong>: Maintains contiguous memory layouts =
throughout processing</p></li>
</ul>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-VRAMConsumptionPatterns">VR=
AM Consumption Patterns</h3>
<p>Gemma-3n represents the largest memory consumer in the model suite, typi=
cally requiring 2-4GB VRAM depending on:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Model precision (bfloat16 vs float32)</p></li>
<li>
<p>Maximum sequence length (determined by token expansion)</p></li>
<li>
<p>Number of input frames (affects pixel_values tensor size)</p></li>
<li>
<p>Batch size (though typically 1 for this deployment)</p></li>
</ul>
<p>The vision encoder and language model weights dominate memory usage, whi=
le activation memory scales with input content size.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-FasterWhisper"><br>
FasterWhisper</h1>
<p>This implementation serves OpenAI's Whisper model through the FasterWhis=
per library, which is a reimplementation optimized for speed and efficiency=
. The model was specifically designed for A/B testing against the Parakeet =
model, featuring identical input/output signatures to enable direct perform=
ance comparisons. The implementation leverages automatic long-form audio ch=
unking via BatchedInferencePipeline and includes conditional gzip compressi=
on for large transcription results.</p>
<p>The implementation provides speech-to-text capabilities through FasterWh=
isper's reimplementation of OpenAI's Whisper model. The focus is on inferen=
ce speed improvements through optimized computation graphs and memory manag=
ement, making it well-suited for production deployment where latency and th=
roughput matter.</p>
<p>The implementation handles feature-length audio content without memory o=
verflow, maintains accuracy across diverse audio conditions, and provides d=
etailed timestamp information for downstream processing workflows.</p>
<p>The implementation uses Whisper's "large-v3" model, which represents the=
 most capable version available at the time of development. Model selection=
 balances accuracy requirements against computational resources, with large=
-v3 providing the best transcription quality for challenging audio content =
including movies, podcasts, and low-quality recordings.</p>
<p>Device selection automatically detects CUDA availability and falls back =
to CPU processing when GPU resources are unavailable. This flexibility ensu=
res the service remains functional even when VRAM is constrained by other m=
odels.</p>
<p>Compute precision defaults to float16 but can be overridden through the =
WHISPER_COMPUTE_TYPE environment variable. Float16 provides significant mem=
ory savings and performance improvements on modern GPUs while maintaining t=
ranscription accuracy for most content. The precision can be adjusted to fl=
oat32 for maximum accuracy when computational resources allow, or to int8 f=
or extreme memory constraints.</p>
<p>The BatchedInferencePipeline represents a significant architectural adva=
ntage over manual chunking approaches. This pipeline automatically handles =
segmentation of long-form audio into manageable chunks, applies overlapping=
 windows to prevent word loss at boundaries, and manages batch processing t=
o maximize GPU utilization.</p>
<p>The pipeline includes built-in Voice Activity Detection (VAD) that autom=
atically identifies speech segments and skips silence or non-speech audio. =
This VAD capability reduces processing time by focusing computation on spee=
ch content while maintaining transcription completeness.</p>
<p>Unlike custom windowing implementations that require manual overlap mana=
gement and boundary condition handling, the pipeline encapsulates best prac=
tices for long-form transcription including optimal chunk sizing and timest=
amp alignment across segments.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Input/OutputTensors.1"><str=
ong>Input/Output Tensors</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Input: </strong><code>AUDIO</code></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: A tensor containing the raw bytes of an au=
dio file.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Input: </strong><code>LANGUAGE</code><strong> (Optional)</strong=
></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: The language of the audio in two-letter IS=
O 639-1 format (e.g., "en", "es"). If omitted, the model will automatically=
 detect the language.</p>
<p></p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Input: </strong><code>TASK</code><strong> (Optional)</strong></p=
>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: Specifies the task, either "transcribe" (d=
efault) or "translate" (to English).</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Output: </strong><code>TRANSCRIPT_JSON</code><strong> / </strong=
><code>TRANSCRIPT_GZIPPED</code></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: A JSON string containing the transcription=
. For payloads over 10KB, the response is gzip compressed and returned in t=
he</p>
<p><code>TRANSCRIPT_GZIPPED</code> tensor. The JSON includes segment-level =
text, timestamps, and word-level details if requested.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Output: </strong><code>LANGUAGE_OUT</code></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: The detected language of the audio.</p></l=
i>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-MediaProcessingPipeline.1">=
<br>
Media Processing Pipeline</h2>
<p>Audio ingestion uses PyAV for universal container format support, handli=
ng mp4, mp3, flac, wav, and other common audio/video formats through a sing=
le code path. The decoder extracts the first available audio stream from an=
y container, eliminating the need for format-specific preprocessing or exte=
rnal conversion tools.</p>
<p>Resampling to 16kHz mono is performed using av.AudioResampler with forma=
t=3D"s16" for 16-bit signed integer output, layout=3D"mono" for single chan=
nel audio, and rate=3D16000 to match Whisper's expected input format. The r=
esampling process includes automatic sample rate conversion from any source=
 rate, channel mixing for multi-channel sources, and format conversion from=
 various bit depths.</p>
<p>The frame-by-frame decoding approach efficiently handles large media fil=
es by processing audio in chunks rather than loading entire files into memo=
ry. This streaming approach prevents memory exhaustion when processing hour=
s of audio content while supporting optional duration limiting for testing =
or preview workflows.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Language">Language</h3>
<p>The implementation supports both automatic language detection and explic=
it language specification through the LANGUAGE input parameter. When no lan=
guage is specified, Whisper's built-in detection examines audio characteris=
tics and linguistic patterns to identify the source language automatically.=
 This detection works reliably for major world languages and many regional =
variants.</p>
<p>Task configuration supports both transcription and translation modes thr=
ough the TASK parameter. Transcription mode produces text in the source lan=
guage, while translation mode converts speech to English text regardless of=
 the input language. This dual capability enables workflows that require ei=
ther faithful transcription or English-language processing of multilingual =
content.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-SegmentandWordProcessing">S=
egment and Word Processing</h3>
<p>The pipeline generates segments that represent natural speech boundaries=
 identified through acoustic and linguistic analysis. Each segment includes=
 start and end timestamps aligned to speech content, transcribed text with =
automatic punctuation and capitalization, and word-level detail when reques=
ted through word_timestamps=3DTrue.</p>
<p>Word-level processing provides granular timing information essential for=
 subtitle generation, audio-visual alignment, and advanced analysis workflo=
ws. Each word object includes the actual text content with whitespace norma=
lized, start and end timestamps rounded to millisecond precision, and confi=
dence scores that indicate transcription certainty.</p>
<p>The confidence scoring mechanism helps identify potentially problematic =
transcriptions where manual review might be beneficial. Low confidence scor=
es often correlate with challenging audio conditions, technical terminology=
, or speech patterns that deviate from training data distributions.</p>
<h1 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-NVIDIAParakeet"><br>
NVIDIA Parakeet</h1>
<p>This implementation serves NVIDIA's Parakeet-TDT-0.6B-v2 ASR model, whic=
h is a NeMo RNNT (Recurrent Neural Network Transducer) with TDT (Token and =
Duration Time) modeling and a FastConformer encoder.</p>
<p>Parakeet-TDT uses an RNNT architecture with a FastConformer encoder. Whi=
le this is not a decoder-only Transformer with full quadratic self-attentio=
n, long audio sequences still stress memory and runtime due to encoder stat=
e management, convolutional subsampling operations, predictor and joint net=
work passes, and timestamp alignment buffer requirements.</p>
<p>Triton hosts models through Python backend classes that expose initializ=
e, execute, and finalize methods. Each Python model process holds its own G=
PU memory allocations. Running multiple Python-backend models with instance=
_group count greater than 1 multiplies VRAM usage by creating multiple copi=
es of model weights.</p>
<p>PyTorch's CUDA caching allocator can cause memory fragmentation where "s=
tale" chunks result in allocation failures even when total free memory appe=
ars sufficient. This requires careful allocator configuration and environme=
nt variable tuning.</p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-Input/OutputTensors.2"><str=
ong>Input/Output Tensors</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Input: </strong><code>AUDIO_PATH</code></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: The absolute filesystem path to the audio =
or video file.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Input: </strong><code>TIMESTAMPS</code><strong> (Optional)</stro=
ng></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: Specifies the desired timestamp granularit=
y. Accepts "word" (default), "segment," or "all".</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Output: </strong><code>TRANSCRIPT_JSON</code><strong> / </strong=
><code>TRANSCRIPT_GZIPPED</code></p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TYPE_STRING</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Description</strong>: A JSON string containing the transcription=
. For payloads over 10KB, the response is gzip compressed and returned in t=
he</p>
<p><code>TRANSCRIPT_GZIPPED</code> tensor. The JSON includes segment-level =
text, timestamps, and word-level details if requested.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<h2 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-MediaProcessingPipeline.2">=
Media Processing Pipeline</h2>
<p>Audio ingestion uses PyAV to decode any container format including mp4, =
mp3, flac, and wav files. The pipeline extracts audio streams, resamples to=
 16kHz mono using av.AudioResampler with format=3D"s16", layout=3D"mono", a=
nd rate=3D16000, then normalizes the resulting int16 data to float32 in the=
 range [-1, 1].</p>
<p>Processing a 4,000-second audio file (equivalent to a 128MB mp3 at 16kHz=
 mono) in a single pass creates enormous intermediate tensors. Even though =
Parakeet doesn't use full quadratic attention, RNNT decoders maintain buffe=
rs and state for each time step while encoders hold activations for each fr=
ame. The encoder's subsampling reduces some memory pressure, but hours of a=
udio still cause memory to balloon beyond available VRAM.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-WindowedInferenceSolution">=
Windowed Inference Solution</h3>
<p>The solution implements windowed or "buffered" inference that slices aud=
io into fixed-size windows of 30 seconds with 0.6 to 1.0 seconds of overlap=
 between adjacent windows. Each window is processed independently through <=
code>model.transcribe()</code>, and the resulting word timestamps are shift=
ed by the window's start offset to create global timestamps.</p>
<p>Overlap deduplication prevents word repetition at window boundaries by d=
ropping any word whose start time is less than <code>(last_end - time_strid=
e)</code>. This approach guarantees bounded GPU memory usage regardless of =
input duration while avoiding word loss at window transitions.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-SegmentationLogic">Segmenta=
tion Logic</h3>
<p>The implementation needed to generate coarse segments for metadata and V=
LM alignment while providing fine-grained words for MAM processing. Rather =
than trusting model-specific "segment" objects that vary across NeMo versio=
ns, segments are built from word timestamps using a simple, configurable po=
licy.</p>
<p>Segmentation begins with the first word and creates new segments when ei=
ther a pause of at least SEGMENT_MIN_GAP_S (default 0.7 seconds) occurs, or=
 when the current segment duration exceeds SEGMENT_MAX_DURATION_S (default =
20 seconds).</p>
<p>To avoid micro-segments, any fragment with fewer than SEGMENT_MIN_WORDS =
words is merged into the previous segment rather than standing alone.</p>
<p>Text reconstruction uses a punctuation-aware joining algorithm that prev=
ents extra spaces around punctuation marks like parentheses and other speci=
al characters.</p>
<p>This approach yields stable start/end time and text blocks without depen=
ding on internal model chunking behavior that may change between versions.<=
/p>
<h2 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-ImplementationNotes">Implem=
entation Notes</h2>
<p>NeMo=E2=80=99s documentation and implementation are often at odds with e=
ach other.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-ModelLoadingIssues">Model L=
oading Issues</h3>
<p>Initial attempts used <code>ASRModel.from_pretrained(model_name=3D"nvidi=
a/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v2", map_location=3Ddevice, cache_dir=3D...)</code> but=
 some NeMo versions reject the cache_dir parameter and raise errors.</p>
<p>The preferred solution calls <code>from_pretrained(model_name=3D..., map=
_location=3D...)</code> without cache_dir and lets HuggingFace environment =
variables like HUGGING_FACE_HUB_CACHE and HF_HOME control caching behavior.=
 The fallback approach downloads the .nemo checkpoint via huggingface_hub a=
nd calls <code>ASRModel.restore_from(restore_path=3D...)</code>.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-TimestampAPIInconsistencies=
">Timestamp API Inconsistencies</h3>
<p>Official documentation suggests two approaches for timestamp generation:=
</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Direct method: <code>asr_model.transcribe(..., timestamps=3DTrue)</code>=
</p></li>
<li>
<p>Configuration method: Set <code>preserve_alignments=3DTrue</code>, <code=
>compute_timestamps=3DTrue</code>, and <code>rnnt_timestamp_type=3D"word"</=
code> in decoding config, then read <code>hyp.timestamp['word']</code> or <=
code>hyp.timestep['word']</code></p></li>
</ul>
<p>In practice, the direct method fails with <code>TypeError: EncDecRNNTMod=
el.transcribe() got an unexpected keyword argument 'timestamps'</code>. The=
 configuration approach triggers warnings like "If compute_timesteps is set=
, then preserve_alignments must also be set."</p>
<p>API variations across versions include different parameter names where s=
ome builds expect <code>compute_timestamps</code> while others use <code>co=
mpute_timesteps</code>. Strategy names can be "greedy_batch" or "greedy". N=
eMo contains intentional typos in some parameter names like "seperator" ins=
tead of "separator".</p>
<p>The production solution builds a defensive decoding configuration that t=
ries parameter variants in sequence while always maintaining <code>preserve=
_alignments=3DTrue</code>, <code>rnnt_timestamp_type=3D"word"</code>, and a=
ppropriate separator settings. The implementation logs which configuration =
variant succeeds for debugging purposes.</p>
<p>Timestamp extraction handles multiple possible attribute names on the hy=
pothesis object including <code>hyp.timestamp</code>, <code>hyp.timestamps<=
/code>, and <code>hyp.timestep</code>. Within each word entry, the code pre=
fers absolute time values like <code>start_time</code> and <code>end_time</=
code> when available, otherwise it maps frame offset values like <code>star=
t_offset</code> and <code>end_offset</code> to seconds using the RNNT strid=
e calculation: <code>time_stride_sec =3D 8 * model.cfg.preprocessor.window_=
stride</code>, which typically equals approximately 0.08 seconds.</p>
<p>Character-level timestamps are intentionally never computed to reduce co=
mputational overhead and memory usage.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-CUDAGraphProblems">CUDA Gra=
ph Problems</h3>
<p>The implementation frequently encountered "CUDA error: an illegal memory=
 access was encountered" errors, often occurring on chunk number 2 during t=
he second processing window.</p>
<p>Root cause analysis revealed that when cuda-python version 12.3 or highe=
r is installed, NeMo attempts to enable CUDA Graphs with conditional nodes =
for the TDT while-loop. Graph capture is extremely sensitive to shape chang=
es, internal memory allocations, and capture-unsafe kernel operations, caus=
ing failures during graph replay.</p>
<p>Mixed precision operations using fp16 can stress fused kernels, particul=
arly with certain GPU driver and CUDA toolkit combinations.</p>
<h3 id=3D"AI-938:NVIDIATritonServerIntergration-StabilityImprovements">Stab=
ility Improvements</h3>
<p>The solution disables CUDA Graphs by uninstalling cuda-python from the T=
riton container image. NeMo will display a benign warning about conditional=
 nodes being unavailable, but this eliminates the graph capture instability=
.</p>
<p>Force fp32 precision without autocast during decode operations to avoid =
fragile mixed-precision kernels that can cause crashes.</p>
<p>Environment configuration includes allocator and debugging settings:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><code>PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=3Dexpandable_segments:True,max_split_size_=
mb:256</code></p></li>
<li>
<p><code>CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=3D1</code> when debugging to get precise erro=
r stack traces</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Future optimizations if CUDA Graphs are revisited could include padding =
the final window to match the length of previous windows so replay shapes r=
emain consistent, and maintaining <code>instance_group.count=3D1</code> to =
avoid multi-process capture interactions.</p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
